24. On why I think this objection is reasonable even though I don’t really provide a great alternative:
Relying on self-reports to a significant extent feels like [streetlight fallacy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Streetlight_effect) to me. I don't think we should update much based on self-reports, especially the type that involves asking people if their whole life has been good or bad rather than looking moment to moment. I don't think I need to provide a better alternative besides intuition / philosophical reasoning to make this claim.
MacAskill does caveat self-reports some but I think the vibe is much more authoritative than I'd prefer, and the caveats much less strongly worded. I'd go for the vibe of "we have no idea wtf is going on here, here's the best empirical evidence we have but DON'T TAKE IT SERIOUSLY AT ALL IT'S REALLY SHITTY" [↩︎](#fnref-dhegXgx5m3cNmiHnr-24)
